<Project>
  <!-- WS-F1-T1 — the single source of the machine-simulator product's version. Every csproj under this
       directory (src/* and tests/*) automatically inherits this via MSBuild's own "walk up from each
       project file to the nearest Directory.Build.props" convention — confirmed no such file exists
       anywhere above this one (repo root included), so nothing here shadows/gets shadowed by a parent.
       St4i.EngineApi.Endpoints.CapabilitiesEndpoints reads this back at runtime off its own built
       assembly's Version (Assembly.GetName().Version, populated from AssemblyVersion below) and reports
       it on GET /v1/capabilities; the installer (a later WS-F1 task) will read this SAME property to
       decide upgrade-vs-fresh-install. Bump this ONE value when the product version changes — every
       assembly + the capabilities endpoint stay in lock-step automatically. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version>1.0.0</Version>
    <AssemblyVersion>1.0.0.0</AssemblyVersion>
    <FileVersion>1.0.0.0</FileVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- 🔴 N-1 (.superpowers/sdd/doc-comments-compiled/task-1-brief.md) — WHY `GenerateDocumentationFile` IS
       SET IN INDIVIDUAL csproj FILES AND DELIBERATELY NOT HERE, where it would reach all fifteen.

       Until N-1 nothing in this repository read a doc comment as SYNTAX. The three instruments that speak
       about comments — the strip-`///` diff, scripts/verify-suites.sh's warning count, and the token
       censuses — all measure TEXT (§8.1(h7)). A `cref` naming a symbol that does not exist, a `paramref`
       naming a parameter that does not exist and a `<para>` that never closes are invisible to every one of
       them BY CONSTRUCTION, which is why §8.1 records cross-assembly crefs rotting for entire batches and
       why J-3 had to build a private XML parser to find four malformed blocks in FleetCore.cs.

       `GenerateDocumentationFile` is the switch that fixes that, and it turns on TWO assertions at once:
         (1) every `///` block PARSES and every symbol it names RESOLVES — CS1570, CS1574, CS0419, CS1734,
             CS1587. This is what N-1 exists to install.
         (2) every publicly visible member HAS a doc comment — CS1591, and one member over, CS1573. This is
             a documentation-COVERAGE policy this product has never adopted anywhere.
       Nothing separates (1) from (2): the mechanisms that would (`NoWarn`, `<NoWarn>`, `#pragma warning
       disable`, an .editorconfig severity, a lowered `WarningLevel`) are all suppression, and suppression is
       precisely how this task fails — it builds the instrument and then blinds it. So (2) is reported as an
       owner decision instead of taken, and the property is set only where (2) already held or was a short
       sitting away from holding.

       THE PRICE OF EACH PROJECT, measured at BASE 811c9054 BEFORE any of it was paid — 2026-08-13, SDK
       10.0.302, `dotnet build -t:Rebuild -p:GenerateDocumentationFile=true` from tools/machine-simulator,
       counted the way MSBuild's own "N Warning(s)" summary counts:

           project                            (1) parse/resolve   (2) CS1591   CS1573   set
           St4i.Connector.Conformance                  15               0          0     yes
           St4i.EdgeCore.Serial                         3               9          0     yes
           St4i.EdgeService                             3               2          0     yes
           St4i.DesktopShell                            0               1          0     yes
           St4i.SerialBench                             0               0          0     yes
           St4i.SettingsAclProbe                        0               0          0     yes
           St4i.Connector.Abstractions                 16              95          0     no
           St4i.Connector.Abstractions.Tests            1             103          0     no
           St4i.Connector.Conformance.Tests             5              51          0     no
           St4i.EdgeService.Tests                       1              44          0     no
           St4iMachineSimulator                        26              57          0     no
           St4i.EdgeCore                              112             543         92     no
           St4i.EngineApi                              53             530        248     no
           St4i.EdgeCore.Tests                         36             705          0     no
           St4i.EngineApi.Tests                        38             742          5     no

       🔴 TWO ROWS ABOVE ARE NOT WHAT THEY LOOK LIKE, because a source file can belong to more than one
       compilation. Neither was visible from the per-project numbers and both were found by review, not by me:
         * St4iMachineSimulator lands TWICE in a solution build — the WPF markup pass compiles a `_wpftmp`
           clone — so setting it there costs 166, not 83.
         * `tests/Shared/TestRunTempRoot.cs` and `tests/Shared/RealCredentialStoreLeakGuard.cs` are
           `Compile`-linked into ALL FIVE test projects, so one doc defect in either counts FIVE times. It is
           not hypothetical: 5 of the 237 below are ONE defect in TestRunTempRoot.cs, which is why the
           distinct-source-site count is 233.
         * And `St4i.EdgeCore` compiles `examples/device-client/csharp/St4iDeviceClient.cs`, linked in from
           OUTSIDE tools/machine-simulator (see St4i.EdgeCore.csproj's `Compile Include`). Its 95 CS1591 and
           8 CS1573 are inside St4i.EdgeCore's row above — and it is a VENDORED reference SDK. That is not an
           assertion, it is in the file's own header: it declares itself the C#/.NET reference client for
           doc 61 (Machine Developer Integration Guide), pins **C# 7.3+ / netstandard2.0+ / .NET Framework
           4.7.2+**, and states feature parity with the Python and Node SDKs — which are its siblings under
           examples/device-client/. It is published to machine developers and kept in step with three other
           implementations; it is not this repository's source to rewrite.

           🔴 THE TWO HALVES OF THAT ARE BLOCKED WITH DIFFERENT STRENGTH, and merging them loses the sharper
           half. Its **82 nullable warnings** (the ones in the gate's 116) are blocked MECHANICALLY: silencing
           them honestly needs nullable ANNOTATIONS (`string?`), annotations need C# 8, and the file's own
           header pins C# 7.3+ — so annotating it would break the compatibility promise it ships with. The
           file carries no `#nullable` directive at all and is analysed under EdgeCore's `enable`, which is
           why the crop is structural rather than accidental. Its **95 CS1591** are blocked only by
           OWNERSHIP: doc comments have been legal since C# 1.0, so writing them is perfectly possible — it
           is simply not our file, and a divergence here is a divergence from three sibling SDKs.
       Read every row above as "this compilation", never as "this directory".

       Column (2) totals 2870 members across the nine unset projects (the twelve in the six set projects
       were WRITTEN, not suppressed, which is why their column-(2) price is now zero). 2870 is the number
       that is an owner decision and not an implementer one, and it is why those nine stay unset: the only
       honest ways to set them are to write 2870 doc comments or to disable a code, and the first is a
       product decision about what those surfaces promise while the second is forbidden here. A doc comment
       invented for a domain field nobody has decided the meaning of is a new lie, not a fix.

       🔴 AND "DISABLE A CODE" IS TWO DIFFERENT DECISIONS THAT DIFFER BY 448 MEMBERS. Both are forbidden to
       an implementer here; they are NOT interchangeable to whoever authorises one:
         (a) ASSEMBLY-WIDE — `<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>` on St4i.EdgeCore. Silences all 543. That
             project then asserts documentation coverage NOWHERE, including on the 448 members that are our
             own source and that nothing prevents anyone from documenting.
         (b) PATH-SCOPED — an .editorconfig section matching only the vendored file
             (`dotnet_diagnostic.CS1591.severity = none` under a pattern for St4iDeviceClient.cs). Silences
             95 and LEAVES THE OTHER 448 ASSERTED, which is the whole difference: it exempts the file nobody
             here may edit and keeps the check on everything we own.
       (b) is strictly the narrower instrument and is what "St4i.EdgeCore is blocked" actually argues for.
       🔴 One thing to settle before relying on it, flagged because I did NOT measure it: this file enters
       the compilation through a `Compile Include` from OUTSIDE the project cone, and .editorconfig sections
       match a file's REAL path, not its `Link` metadata — so such a section has to be scoped to the real
       path under examples/device-client/csharp/, and that should be verified by running it rather than
       reasoned about. Everything else in this block was measured; that sentence was not.

       🔴 AND IF ONLY ONE PROJECT EVER MOVES, MOVE St4i.Connector.Abstractions. Put here rather than in a
       report because this is where someone deciding will be standing. Three facts, and they point the same
       way for once:
         * It is the ONLY documented surface in this tree with readers outside this organisation — it is the
           contract a third-party driver author compiles against and reads first (zero PackageReference, zero
           ProjectReference, deliberately, so they can).
         * By the argument this whole block makes, it is therefore the surface whose doc-comment correctness
           matters MOST — and the scope above leaves it unguarded. The six were selected by what assertion (2)
           COST, and cost correlates only loosely with what a comment's correctness is worth. This row is
           where the two come apart hardest, and it is the honest weakness of the scope, not a footnote to it.
         * It is also the CHEAPEST such move — 95 members, the smallest column-(2) bill of the nine — and the
           only one measured to be UNBLOCKED: every one of those 95 is our own source, so unlike
           St4i.EdgeCore no part of the bill sits in a file we may not edit. Its column (1) is already ZERO
           (N-1 fixed all 16 there even though the switch is off, precisely so this stayed a one-line change).
       So the whole cost of guarding the most-read surface in the repository is: write 95 doc comments, add
       one property. Nothing else has to be decided first.

       Column (1) is a different thing entirely — every one of those is a claim that is already FALSE. N-1
       fixed all 21 inside the six projects above (33 warnings in total there, with column (2)), which is why
       setting the property moved scripts/verify-suites.sh's EXPECT_WARNINGS by ZERO. It also fixed, tree-wide
       and outside the six, the two sets §8.1 names by measurement — St4i.Connector.Abstractions' 16 and
       FleetCore.cs's four malformed blocks — plus EVERY remaining malformed-XML block anywhere in the tree
       (19 blocks in 15 files under tools/machine-simulator, plus one block misplaced onto a record parameter
       in a 16th — CS1570 and CS1587 are now zero in every one of the fifteen compilations, which includes the
       linked-in vendored file: it was scanned, and it is clean), because that is the class J-3 built a
       private parser to find and it is now empty. 237 unresolvable `cref`/`paramref` claims remain in the
       nine unset projects, listed by file in .superpowers/sdd/doc-comments-compiled/task-1-report.md and NOT
       fixed: a fix there is guarded by nothing and witnessed by nothing until the switch can be set.

       🔴 A malformed block HIDES the diagnostics inside it — measured here, not assumed. Closing the 19
       blocks made the compiler report EIGHT further defects it had previously abandoned the block before
       reaching: 4 CS1573 and 1 CS1574 in RtuBusConfiguration, 3 CS1734 in ConnectorConfigVisibilitySeeder.
       Fix the parse errors first; the resolve errors underneath them are not visible until you do. (A ninth
       change is not of that kind and is called out so the arithmetic is not mistaken for it: ConfigDtos.cs
       carried a `///` block on a positional record parameter, where C# attaches it to nothing at all — CS1587.
       Moving it to a real `<param>` on the record made it live documentation and made 24 CS1573 sayable, which
       is the true statement "this record documents one parameter of twenty-five".)

       To re-run the measurement without changing anything, from tools/machine-simulator:
           dotnet build -t:Rebuild -p:GenerateDocumentationFile=true
       That command turns the whole tree on for one build and is the diagnostic that replaces J-3's
       purpose-built parser. -->
</Project>
